# Task: Implement Lazy<T> Wrapper Type for Deferred Loading

## Context
Lazy<T> enables deferred/lazy loading of related objects. Unlike Related<T> (eager loading), Lazy<T> only fetches the related object when accessed, requiring a Session reference.

## Design Decisions
1. **Requires Session**: Must have active session to load
2. **Interior mutability**: Uses OnceCell to cache loaded value
3. **No implicit DB access**: User must call load() explicitly in Rust
4. **Transparent get**: After loading, access is fast

## Why Different from Related<T>?
- **Related<T>**: Loaded at query time via JOIN, no Session needed after
- **Lazy<T>**: Loaded on-demand, requires Session, can cause N+1 if misused

## What to Implement

### 1. Lazy<T> Type
\`\`\`rust
use std::cell::OnceCell;

/// A lazily-loaded related object.
/// 
/// Requires a Session to load the related object on first access.
#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct Lazy<T: Model> {
    /// Foreign key value (if any)
    fk_value: Option<Value>,
    /// Loaded object (cached after first load)
    loaded: OnceCell<Option<T>>,
    /// Whether load() has been called
    load_attempted: bool,
}

impl<T: Model> Lazy<T> {
    pub const fn empty() -> Self {
        Self {
            fk_value: None,
            loaded: OnceCell::new(),
            load_attempted: false,
        }
    }

    pub fn from_fk(fk: impl Into<Value>) -> Self {
        Self {
            fk_value: Some(fk.into()),
            loaded: OnceCell::new(),
            load_attempted: false,
        }
    }

    /// Load the related object from the database.
    /// Returns the loaded object or None if FK is null.
    pub async fn load(&mut self, session: &mut Session) -> Result<Option<&T>> {
        if self.load_attempted {
            return Ok(self.loaded.get().and_then(|o| o.as_ref()));
        }

        self.load_attempted = true;

        let Some(fk) = &self.fk_value else {
            let _ = self.loaded.set(None);
            return Ok(None);
        };

        let obj = session.get::<T>(fk.clone()).await?;
        let _ = self.loaded.set(obj);

        Ok(self.loaded.get().and_then(|o| o.as_ref()))
    }

    /// Get the loaded object (None if not yet loaded or FK is null).
    pub fn get(&self) -> Option<&T> {
        self.loaded.get().and_then(|o| o.as_ref())
    }

    /// Check if load() has been called.
    pub fn is_loaded(&self) -> bool {
        self.load_attempted
    }

    /// Check if FK is null (no related object possible).
    pub fn is_empty(&self) -> bool {
        self.fk_value.is_none()
    }

    /// Get the foreign key value.
    pub fn fk(&self) -> Option<&Value> {
        self.fk_value.as_ref()
    }
}
\`\`\`

### 2. Batch Loading (N+1 Prevention)
\`\`\`rust
impl Session {
    /// Load multiple lazy relationships in a single query.
    /// Prevents N+1 by batching FK values.
    pub async fn load_many<T, U>(&mut self, 
        objects: &mut [T], 
        accessor: impl Fn(&mut T) -> &mut Lazy<U>
    ) -> Result<()> 
    where 
        T: Model,
        U: Model,
    {
        // Collect all FK values
        let fks: Vec<Value> = objects.iter()
            .filter_map(|o| accessor(o).fk().cloned())
            .collect();

        if fks.is_empty() {
            return Ok(());
        }

        // Single query for all related objects
        let related = select!(U)
            .filter(U::pk_column().is_in(&fks))
            .all(self)
            .await?;

        // Build FK -> object lookup
        let lookup: HashMap<Value, U> = related
            .into_iter()
            .map(|o| (o.primary_key_value()[0].clone(), o))
            .collect();

        // Populate each Lazy<U>
        for obj in objects {
            let lazy = accessor(obj);
            if let Some(fk) = lazy.fk() {
                let related = lookup.get(fk).cloned();
                let _ = lazy.loaded.set(related);
                lazy.load_attempted = true;
            }
        }

        Ok(())
    }
}
