# Task: Implement Related<T> Wrapper Type

## Context
Related<T> is the core wrapper type for eager-loaded single-object relationships (ManyToOne, OneToOne). It holds either the loaded object or the foreign key for deferred resolution.

## Design Rationale
Why a wrapper type instead of just T?
1. **State tracking**: Know if loaded, not-loaded, or null
2. **FK storage**: Keep FK value even before loading
3. **Type safety**: Different type for loaded vs potential-to-load
4. **Serialization**: Can serialize FK without loading related object

## What to Implement

### 1. Related<T> Type
\`\`\`rust
// In sqlmodel-core/src/relationship.rs

use std::cell::OnceCell;

/// A related single object (many-to-one or one-to-one).
/// 
/// This type can be in one of three states:
/// - Empty: No relationship (FK is null)
/// - Unloaded: Has FK value but object not fetched
/// - Loaded: Object has been fetched and cached
#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct Related<T: Model> {
    /// The foreign key value (if any)
    fk_value: Option<Value>,

    /// The loaded object (if fetched)
    loaded: OnceCell<Option<T>>,
}

impl<T: Model> Related<T> {
    /// Create an empty relationship (null FK)
    pub const fn empty() -> Self {
        Self {
            fk_value: None,
            loaded: OnceCell::new(),
        }
    }

    /// Create from a foreign key value (not yet loaded)
    pub fn from_fk(fk: impl Into<Value>) -> Self {
        Self {
            fk_value: Some(fk.into()),
            loaded: OnceCell::new(),
        }
    }

    /// Create with an already-loaded object
    pub fn loaded(obj: T) -> Self {
        let cell = OnceCell::new();
        let _ = cell.set(Some(obj));
        Self {
            fk_value: None,
            loaded: cell,
        }
    }

    /// Get the loaded object (None if not loaded or null FK)
    pub fn get(&self) -> Option<&T> {
        self.loaded.get().and_then(|o| o.as_ref())
    }

    /// Check if the relationship has been loaded
    pub fn is_loaded(&self) -> bool {
        self.loaded.get().is_some()
    }

    /// Check if the relationship is empty (null FK)
    pub fn is_empty(&self) -> bool {
        self.fk_value.is_none()
    }

    /// Get the foreign key value
    pub fn fk(&self) -> Option<&Value> {
        self.fk_value.as_ref()
    }

    /// Set the loaded object (internal use by query system)
    pub fn set_loaded(&self, obj: Option<T>) -> Result<(), Option<T>> {
        self.loaded.set(obj)
    }
}
